hi I'm Mai Yuko and I'm a yappy now if  you haven't heard of this term before  yeah P stands for young Asian  professional we're known to pursue  financially comfortable jobs not take  huge risks in life and overall just play  it safe I honestly don't think that it's  a bad thing to be happy nor is it  probably like a great thing either  either way I think this is the life that  my parents and grandparents worked so  hard for so I like to think that I'm  making them proud wait hold up but don't  they want you to do meaningful work that  impacts the lives of others well yeah  but I'm just kind of doing that through  like a well-paying job but in this day  and age don't you take risks to do  what's right for yourself and your  community even if it compromises our  financial independence because it's just  the right thing to do well maybe so  here's my story from a young age my  parents encouraged me to find a job  that's financially stable it makes sense  my parents were immigrants from Japan  who didn't have a whole lot to fall back  on as they raised me and my brother  while they were finishing grad school  the fear of the unknown the fear of  living as an immigrant in this country  where they had no roots challenged us  and meant that we needed to stay strong  to support each other not that things  ever felt super rough for me growing up  I was born and raised here and I spoke  English fluently so I didn't feel much  different from the white kid who was  sitting next to me it felt like I had a  pretty normal childhood until maybe  about high school came around and I  thought about cultural identity issues  but we'll talk about that in another  video  anyways I knew that my parents struggles  I would have to make it on my own so I  went to college instead computer science  to become a software engineer that was  like my goal in life for 21 years  go to school graduate and get a job it  was a straightforward plan to me and it  was simple but it turns out that when  you're striving for a milestone and not  a mission you can feel lost when you  achieve it so after I got a job and the  whole honeymoon period of working 9 to 5  wore off I found myself asking now what  was I going to be working 40 hour weeks  as a software engineer for the rest of  my life I mean sure that sounded ok  considering I never would have to worry  about money again  was I ever going to do something that  was sooo  we're out there and risk everything that  I had worked so hard to get yeah no  there was no way I was giving that up  but did I want to find a greater purpose  and meaning in life and figure out who I  am as a person all my values are so that  I could have a positive impact on the  world well yeah definitely but I feel  like I was afraid of something why  couldn't I just do it what was holding  me back well it turns out that it's the  same fear that kept my family together  the fear of the unknown the uncertainty  of making it through living in a country  that I called home once I realize this I  realized that every decision of my life  was fueled by a strong sense of worry  and fear I mean like going to CSD  because I didn't want to be far away  from my family  what about studying computer science the  job prospects were good and I was good  at math and science deciding to be a  software engineer well the pay isn't bad  these decisions were all fueled by my  worries would my family be okay would I  have any regrets in life will I be able  to spend enough time with my family but  also these questions make me realize  what's important to me and what I want  in life I think that maybe the emotion  of fear was a stronger emotion than  anything else and you know the more that  I think about it the more that I think  that this way of living is totally okay  and that it's okay to keep asking myself  these questions frequently I mean I  never want to get to a point where I'm  not putting myself or my family first or  putting ourselves in danger it's just  like not part of my nature to do  something like that but it is safe and  safe might not get me to the type of  life that I want to live you see my  biggest fear in life is dying without  having done all the things that I wanted  to do in life when I asked myself what  those things actually were I found that  some of those things were tangible and  milestone oriented getting a job  adopting a dog getting married having  kids but I also realized that I have  some intangible goals too like having an  incredible human experience and feeling  a wide range of human emotions or  getting to know all kinds of people and  very Millenial of me but having a  positive  impact on the world I want to cry about  things that I didn't even know I could  cry about and of course laughing until I  cried like a million more times and to  be honest I'm at a point in my life  where a lot of those tangible goals  luckily are doable but I have like no  real idea on how to achieve my  intangible goals  honestly I couldn't even put into words  what this goal were until recently  because just the uncertainty used to  scare me so much but realizing what I  want from life is forcing me to come to  terms with the fact that I need to put  myself in challenging and difficult  situations that I wouldn't have put  myself in before and I know that I'm not  quite ready to make like big risky moves  in life like I'm still gonna make very  responsible decisions actually making  videos is one way that I'm doing this  and I don't think I'll be making videos  necessarily like a safe route it forces  me to look at myself and understand Who  I am as an individual so that I can  positively impact others but yeah I'm  still very much EAP and I think there's  nothing wrong with that there is nothing  wrong with playing it safe you gotta do  what you gotta do but I also feel like  it's a waste to not try to understand  who you are why you are and what you're  gonna do about it we only have one life  on this earth and if you think about the  vastness of the universe and how  miniscule your life is in comparison to  everything else then you have a  responsibility to live it so that it  felt significant to you the person that  matters most  [Music]
 

